WEBVTT

﻿1
00:00:07.007 --> 00:00:12.094
People would fly high above the crowd.
just flipping. like. end over end.

2
00:00:12.095 --> 00:00:15.139
like... [imitates flying sound]

3
00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:19.018
and into the arms of a person
waiting on the other side.

4
00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:21.604
[breathes heavily]
Isn't that wild?

5
00:00:21.605 --> 00:00:22.605
[Juliette] Mm-hmm.

6
00:00:22.606 --> 00:00:24.942
[breathes heavily]

7
00:00:25.526 --> 00:00:30.363
Oh. another thing about the
circus. they had animals

8
00:00:30.364 --> 00:00:32.990
and they had these huge
things called elephants

9
00:00:32.991 --> 00:00:36.202
and they had. like... like.
skin as hard as concrete

10
00:00:36.203 --> 00:00:40.040
and these long. flexible
noses that they called trunks.

11
00:00:41.166 --> 00:00:47.166
and teeth that grew on either side of
their faces could grow up to 12 feet long.

12
00:00:47.256 --> 00:00:48.856
[Juliette] That sounds
like a nightmare.

13
00:00:49.633 --> 00:00:51.134
[Solo] Yeah. I like elephants.

14
00:00:52.344 --> 00:00:53.469
[sighs]

15
00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:55.264
[Solo] They're really smart.

16
00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.391
They're very altruistic.

17
00:00:58.392 --> 00:01:01.978
You know. th-they've been known
to help other species in trouble.

18
00:01:01.979 --> 00:01:04.939
Well. right now I'm
a species in trouble.

19
00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:05.898
What?

20
00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:08.860
[inhales sharply] I want to try and
breathe through 50 feet of tubing

21
00:01:08.861 --> 00:01:12.655
without having to make
something to push the air down.

22
00:01:12.656 --> 00:01:13.949
But I don't think I can.

23
00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:16.034
Can you take that?

24
00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.287
And...

25
00:01:19.663 --> 00:01:21.790
I think I can fix it. [sighs]

26
00:01:23.792 --> 00:01:26.169
Hey. you're pretty good
at making things. huh?

27
00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:27.254
[Juliette] Mm-hmm.

28
00:01:27.963 --> 00:01:29.714
So. what was your
job at your silo?

29
00:01:29.715 --> 00:01:32.175
- Hey. could you... Just lo... Thank you.
- Oh. sorry. Um.

30
00:01:32.176 --> 00:01:35.136
[Juliette] Um. I
was an engineer.

31
00:01:35.137 --> 00:01:36.430
Oh.

32
00:01:37.264 --> 00:01:41.101
Hey. you know. they... they used machines
to breathe underwater in the before times.

33
00:01:41.643 --> 00:01:42.685
[sucks teeth]

34
00:01:42.686 --> 00:01:46.230
Oh. there's this great book called
<i>Twenty Thousand Leagues Under the Sea.</i>

35
00:01:46.231 --> 00:01:51.110
and in it they use breathing equipment
and this massive underwater ship

36
00:01:51.111 --> 00:01:53.571
called the <i>Nautilus</i> to
explore the ocean floor.

37
00:01:53.572 --> 00:01:55.406
The ocean was this vast
expanse of salt wa...

38
00:01:55.407 --> 00:01:56.992
No. I know. I... I've seen it.

39
00:01:58.076 --> 00:02:02.039
- I've seen it in a book.
- They let you read books in 18?

40
00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:05.375
Uh. no.

41
00:02:05.876 --> 00:02:07.628
Oh. Ah. man.

42
00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:10.254
Wait. Where was I? Oh. yeah.

43
00:02:10.255 --> 00:02:13.090
In the book. the... the...
the captain of the <i>Nautilus</i>

44
00:02:13.091 --> 00:02:14.217
- is Nemo...
- No. [sighs]

45
00:02:14.218 --> 00:02:16.698
- and you're a little bit like him.
- Just... Can you just...

46
00:02:17.513 --> 00:02:18.597
Sorry.

47
00:02:19.848 --> 00:02:20.849
[sighs]

48
00:02:22.226 --> 00:02:23.227
Hey.

49
00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:25.311
- Hey.
- Yeah?

50
00:02:25.312 --> 00:02:27.188
- You could use a harmonium.
- Hmm?

51
00:02:27.189 --> 00:02:30.316
- It's a musical instrument. [stammers]
- No. I need something to write on.

52
00:02:30.317 --> 00:02:35.321
I need like... [stammers] a piece
of slate or it's... This is not...

53
00:02:35.322 --> 00:02:36.989
There's a chalkboard
in the same room.

54
00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.283
- Come on.
- Okay. okay.

55
00:02:39.284 --> 00:02:41.324
- Let's do that.
- [stammers] Should I grab... Here.

56
00:02:43.038 --> 00:02:46.158
[Knox] When we were kids. I used to think
these were the names of the Founders.

57
00:02:47.835 --> 00:02:51.338
Now. I think they're the people from
Mechanical who died in rebellions.

58
00:02:53.882 --> 00:02:57.051
I think the rebellion we grew up
hearing about was just the last one.

59
00:02:57.052 --> 00:03:00.389
And before that. they
happened every generation.

60
00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:02.849
And you don't want Mechanical
to start another one?

61
00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:04.309
If we started any of them.

62
00:03:06.144 --> 00:03:08.105
Look. all right?

63
00:03:09.523 --> 00:03:11.358
Look. Look what they
wrote next to the names.

64
00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.904
"Started with food
shortage in the farms."

65
00:03:17.072 --> 00:03:21.535
And then... It looks like.
"Blamed on Mechanical."

66
00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:24.705
{\an8}You see?

67
00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:28.584
"Factory workers shut down."

68
00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:35.257
"Trash chute fire."

69
00:03:35.966 --> 00:03:39.011
And look at this. Look.
it's hard to see. but...

70
00:03:40.429 --> 00:03:44.140
"No matter where
rebellion started.

71
00:03:44.141 --> 00:03:47.602
the finger always
pointed at Mechanical."

72
00:03:47.603 --> 00:03:48.854
Why?

73
00:03:50.522 --> 00:03:51.523
Why is that?

74
00:03:53.692 --> 00:03:55.777
Because we can shut
this fucker down.

75
00:03:57.112 --> 00:03:58.113
That's right.

76
00:03:59.489 --> 00:04:00.929
[Shirley] You're
not ready for that.

77
00:04:01.283 --> 00:04:02.408
Us shutting it down?

78
00:04:02.409 --> 00:04:05.286
If it gets us what we really need
and we've exhausted all of our...

79
00:04:05.287 --> 00:04:07.372
- They killed Cooper.
- I know!

80
00:04:11.293 --> 00:04:13.003
And they sent
Jules out to clean.

81
00:04:14.463 --> 00:04:18.175
But. Shirl. other than
revenge. what do we want?

82
00:04:21.136 --> 00:04:22.179
[sighs]

83
00:04:24.056 --> 00:04:25.098
The truth.

84
00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:30.187
So let's fucking demand it.

85
00:04:33.315 --> 00:04:34.650
I'm the head of Mechanical.

86
00:04:35.484 --> 00:04:37.027
I can get a meeting
with the mayor.

87
00:04:37.528 --> 00:04:38.528
We take a small group.

88
00:04:38.529 --> 00:04:41.532
You. me. Walker. maybe
McLain. the head of Supply.

89
00:04:42.616 --> 00:04:43.617
And we go up.

90
00:04:45.118 --> 00:04:47.078
No crowds. no weapons.

91
00:04:47.079 --> 00:04:50.164
Every step we take will be
considered an act of aggression.

92
00:04:50.165 --> 00:04:52.667
Which is why we send a
message to the mayor first.

93
00:04:52.668 --> 00:04:54.377
Laying out exactly what
we want to talk about.

94
00:04:54.378 --> 00:04:55.462
Not the mayor.

95
00:04:56.171 --> 00:04:57.171
The judge.

96
00:04:57.172 --> 00:04:59.215
Jules told Walker that
Meadows is an empty robe.

97
00:04:59.216 --> 00:05:01.175
But the Silo doesn't know that.

98
00:05:01.176 --> 00:05:05.138
As far as everyone's concerned.
Judge Meadows is the boss.

99
00:05:06.515 --> 00:05:07.516
Okay.

100
00:05:08.308 --> 00:05:09.560
Okay?

101
00:05:10.227 --> 00:05:11.520
I'll send her the message.

102
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:19.074
Advertise your product or brand here
contact www.OpenSubtitles.org today

103
00:06:47.199 --> 00:06:48.950
- [door opens]
- Is it true a gang from Mechanical

104
00:06:48.951 --> 00:06:50.493
is going to meet
with Judge Meadows?

105
00:06:50.494 --> 00:06:52.036
- Hardly a gang.
- Let me stop them.

106
00:06:52.037 --> 00:06:54.247
- I have 50 raiders on 110.
- No. No. no. Rob.

107
00:06:54.248 --> 00:06:56.207
We're gonna let them
air their grievances.

108
00:06:56.208 --> 00:06:58.084
Maybe it'll bring
down the temperature.

109
00:06:58.085 --> 00:07:00.127
The ultimate goal is
to stop a rebellion.

110
00:07:00.128 --> 00:07:01.587
- I don't care how.
- Every level they climb

111
00:07:01.588 --> 00:07:03.005
is one level closer
to the air lock.

112
00:07:03.006 --> 00:07:04.757
What happens if they
rush the stairs?

113
00:07:04.758 --> 00:07:06.092
Two of this gang are old ladies.

114
00:07:06.093 --> 00:07:08.386
They're not gonna make
a run for the air lock.

115
00:07:08.387 --> 00:07:11.264
But put some raiders above
Judicial if you want.

116
00:07:11.265 --> 00:07:13.892
This isn't the only reason
the judge concerns me.

117
00:07:14.393 --> 00:07:16.228
She also agreed to
meet with Lukas Kyle.

118
00:07:16.728 --> 00:07:19.856
He requested a hearing about his sentence.
If she let's him go. that's not good.

119
00:07:19.857 --> 00:07:21.577
I agree. What do you
want me to do about it?

120
00:07:22.192 --> 00:07:24.194
I was hoping you could
have a word with her.

121
00:07:25.737 --> 00:07:27.239
I know you've been
talking to her.

122
00:07:30.617 --> 00:07:31.743
You've been tracking that?

123
00:07:33.203 --> 00:07:34.788
Just an observation.

124
00:07:36.707 --> 00:07:41.128
Well. the next time I speak with her.
I'll be sure to raise your concerns.

125
00:07:47.259 --> 00:07:48.759
You agreed to meet
with some Mechanicals?

126
00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:50.553
They're citizens of the Silo who
want to speak to their judge.

127
00:07:50.554 --> 00:07:54.432
- It sends a bad message.
- No. Bernard. it sends a new message.

128
00:07:54.433 --> 00:07:55.975
You also agreed to hear
Lukas Kyle's petition?

129
00:07:55.976 --> 00:07:57.476
He has a right to
request a review.

130
00:07:57.477 --> 00:07:59.730
And you have a
right to refuse it.

131
00:08:00.689 --> 00:08:02.940
What are you so afraid of?

132
00:08:02.941 --> 00:08:05.141
- He's dangerous.
- Well. now I really want to meet him.

133
00:08:05.736 --> 00:08:07.236
- I'm being serious.
- [Meadows] No. you're not.

134
00:08:07.237 --> 00:08:11.992
Because if you were being serious. you
would have brought my cleaning suit.

135
00:08:13.702 --> 00:08:15.620
- Do you have it?
- [sighs] It's being assembled!

136
00:08:15.621 --> 00:08:18.247
Well. then when you have
my suit. we can talk.

137
00:08:18.248 --> 00:08:20.666
[sucks teeth. sighs]

138
00:08:20.667 --> 00:08:21.960
[door opens]

139
00:08:29.551 --> 00:08:31.637
[Juliette] Hey. what...
what are all these numbers?

140
00:08:33.429 --> 00:08:36.390
- [Solo] Uh... no idea.
- Okay. I'm gonna need some room.

141
00:08:38.143 --> 00:08:40.812
Erase them. I don't
think anyone will mind.

142
00:08:48.487 --> 00:08:49.780
Ah.

143
00:08:53.992 --> 00:08:55.369
Ah.

144
00:08:57.162 --> 00:08:58.830
Founders' Day.

145
00:08:59.998 --> 00:09:03.085
[Solo] It was a week before
Founders' Day that it all happened.

146
00:09:05.003 --> 00:09:09.382
Hey. did... did you have
Founders' Day in... in your silo?

147
00:09:09.383 --> 00:09:10.383
[Juliette] No.

148
00:09:11.093 --> 00:09:13.387
[Solo] Did you... Did
you have any holidays?

149
00:09:14.179 --> 00:09:15.763
[Juliette] We had Freedom Day.

150
00:09:15.764 --> 00:09:17.306
What did that celebrate?

151
00:09:17.307 --> 00:09:18.934
Freedom. Solo.

152
00:09:19.434 --> 00:09:20.685
Oh. yeah.

153
00:09:20.686 --> 00:09:21.770
Sorry.

154
00:09:23.605 --> 00:09:26.900
[scribbling]

155
00:09:32.364 --> 00:09:33.782
[sighs]

156
00:09:39.454 --> 00:09:40.873
[Solo sighs]

157
00:09:41.456 --> 00:09:42.666
Lapis...

158
00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:47.421
She used to sit next to me.

159
00:09:48.213 --> 00:09:49.922
She was a funny girl.

160
00:09:49.923 --> 00:09:53.427
She was always making
noises and cracking me up.

161
00:09:54.011 --> 00:09:55.053
[chuckles]

162
00:10:00.726 --> 00:10:01.852
Her mom...

163
00:10:03.979 --> 00:10:06.023
she would jar peach preserves.

164
00:10:07.107 --> 00:10:10.651
She said that her name
meant blue or something.

165
00:10:10.652 --> 00:10:12.778
But I think she just said that

166
00:10:12.779 --> 00:10:15.114
- because that was her favorite color...
- [exhales sharply]

167
00:10:15.115 --> 00:10:17.409
and she really
liked my blue eyes.

168
00:10:19.494 --> 00:10:20.495
[Juliette] Solo?

169
00:10:21.997 --> 00:10:26.125
- This is. um. a Level B classroom?
- Mm-hmm.

170
00:10:26.126 --> 00:10:30.756
So she would have been 11 or
12 when the rebellion started.

171
00:10:31.882 --> 00:10:33.509
So. how were you
sitting next to her?

172
00:10:36.512 --> 00:10:40.349
Well. I... I didn't mean
that this belonged to Lapis.

173
00:10:41.725 --> 00:10:42.935
Hmm.

174
00:10:45.771 --> 00:10:47.021
What happened to your eye?

175
00:10:47.022 --> 00:10:48.898
Was that during
the rebellion or...

176
00:10:48.899 --> 00:10:51.276
Yeah. that's some... something
I don't like to talk about.

177
00:10:52.569 --> 00:10:55.112
[gasps] Do... I forgot.

178
00:10:55.113 --> 00:10:56.614
Did you... Do you see this?

179
00:10:56.615 --> 00:10:58.824
Have you seen this or
something like this?

180
00:10:58.825 --> 00:11:00.225
[stammers] Can I...
Can I show you?

181
00:11:01.495 --> 00:11:03.895
[Kathleen] I can pack more ginger
in case you're gone longer.

182
00:11:04.414 --> 00:11:05.998
- You're worried.
- [Kathleen] Of course I am.

183
00:11:05.999 --> 00:11:08.544
There's a mob from
Mechanical on their way up.

184
00:11:09.253 --> 00:11:13.257
It's only a few people the judge
agreed to see and I trust her.

185
00:11:16.093 --> 00:11:18.262
If you want. I can
ask a deputy to stay.

186
00:11:19.847 --> 00:11:21.306
I don't want a deputy.

187
00:11:22.891 --> 00:11:23.892
I want you.

188
00:11:26.895 --> 00:11:29.105
If I can help keep
the peace down there.

189
00:11:29.106 --> 00:11:31.358
then none of us will have
anything to worry about.

190
00:11:32.693 --> 00:11:37.364
I'll ask somebody to stop by and
I will come back as soon as I can.

191
00:11:39.449 --> 00:11:40.449
Okay?

192
00:11:43.662 --> 00:11:44.830
[kisses]

193
00:11:53.672 --> 00:11:56.049
[cart clattering]

194
00:12:10.981 --> 00:12:11.981
You get them?

195
00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:15.819
My dogs were pissed.

196
00:12:16.486 --> 00:12:17.487
But yeah...

197
00:12:18.864 --> 00:12:19.865
I got 'em.

198
00:12:21.366 --> 00:12:22.366
You want to see?

199
00:12:22.367 --> 00:12:24.036
I know red from green.

200
00:12:24.536 --> 00:12:26.162
[clicks tongue] Okay. then.

201
00:12:26.163 --> 00:12:28.498
You could get literally
anyone to do this.

202
00:12:29.708 --> 00:12:30.709
Let me come with you.

203
00:12:31.919 --> 00:12:32.920
For Coop.

204
00:12:35.130 --> 00:12:36.173
We said four people.

205
00:12:36.715 --> 00:12:37.716
Not five.

206
00:12:38.258 --> 00:12:39.384
[Carla] I could stay here.

207
00:12:39.885 --> 00:12:43.305
Sleep in my own bed. Catch
you guys on the way back down.

208
00:12:45.015 --> 00:12:46.183
Suit yourself.

209
00:12:48.769 --> 00:12:51.063
[Camille] Meadows
wasn't always a drunk.

210
00:12:51.813 --> 00:12:54.608
When I was in Judicial. I
heard stories about her mind.

211
00:12:55.108 --> 00:12:56.568
The best there ever was.

212
00:12:57.778 --> 00:13:00.531
Then... [sighs] ...I don't
know. Something happened.

213
00:13:01.114 --> 00:13:02.240
I never heard what.

214
00:13:02.241 --> 00:13:03.492
I had a thought.

215
00:13:04.576 --> 00:13:06.327
This might be a step too far.

216
00:13:06.328 --> 00:13:10.541
but the Pact outlines three
grounds for impeaching a judge.

217
00:13:11.166 --> 00:13:14.293
Proven misbehavior.
incapacity to act.

218
00:13:14.294 --> 00:13:16.337
- or endangering the Silo.
- Endangering the Silo.

219
00:13:16.338 --> 00:13:17.464
Impeachment?

220
00:13:18.382 --> 00:13:22.553
- That could be very destabilizing.
- Or it could unite the upper half.

221
00:13:23.345 --> 00:13:26.723
I'll need support from a majority
of levels and departments.

222
00:13:28.016 --> 00:13:30.518
I don't know if there's time to gather
enough signatures to stop the meeting.

223
00:13:30.519 --> 00:13:31.559
You don't need signatures.

224
00:13:32.104 --> 00:13:36.233
You only need the threat of impeachment
to send a message to Bernard.

225
00:13:37.359 --> 00:13:38.860
He's the one to sideline her.

226
00:13:38.861 --> 00:13:41.905
If he does. maybe he goes
back to seeking your counsel.

227
00:13:46.535 --> 00:13:48.537
[music playing]

228
00:13:57.129 --> 00:13:58.881
Solo. could you just stop?

229
00:13:59.631 --> 00:14:02.133
- Stop.
- No. no. no. You got... You gotta listen.

230
00:14:02.134 --> 00:14:04.594
I'm trying to figure this
out and you're playing music.

231
00:14:04.595 --> 00:14:05.970
And it's just in my head.

232
00:14:05.971 --> 00:14:08.431
Yeah but how do you think
I'm playing the music?

233
00:14:08.432 --> 00:14:09.683
I don't care!

234
00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:12.895
[music stops]

235
00:14:15.105 --> 00:14:16.106
Yes. you do.

236
00:14:20.319 --> 00:14:22.904
Just... I'm trying to
show you something.

237
00:14:22.905 --> 00:14:24.447
Will you just come
over here. please?

238
00:14:24.448 --> 00:14:26.449
Just one time and I
won't mention it again.

239
00:14:26.450 --> 00:14:27.576
I promise.

240
00:14:35.125 --> 00:14:36.325
- [Juliette] Just play.
- Okay.

241
00:14:38.921 --> 00:14:40.923
[music playing]

242
00:14:46.386 --> 00:14:48.180
No. not my hands.
Look at my feet.

243
00:14:50.599 --> 00:14:51.682
- [Juliette] Yeah.
- See?

244
00:14:51.683 --> 00:14:54.602
There... There's a bellows inside
that pushes air through a valve.

245
00:14:54.603 --> 00:14:57.105
You could use this to
push air down a tube.

246
00:15:00.567 --> 00:15:01.568
Look. look.

247
00:15:16.375 --> 00:15:18.085
[Juliette] I'm gonna
have to take it apart.

248
00:15:21.380 --> 00:15:22.755
- [music stops]
- What?

249
00:15:22.756 --> 00:15:25.633
Yeah. but you could put it
back together again. right?

250
00:15:25.634 --> 00:15:27.802
I don't... I don't know. Maybe.

251
00:15:27.803 --> 00:15:30.721
Why don't you just play
one last time. okay?

252
00:15:30.722 --> 00:15:32.807
- Just...
- Oh. man.

253
00:15:32.808 --> 00:15:35.435
Well. we'll see.

254
00:15:35.978 --> 00:15:38.187
[music playing]

255
00:15:38.188 --> 00:15:41.566
[Meadows] When a team of agents showed
up at Nichols's apartment. you fled.

256
00:15:41.567 --> 00:15:42.859
- Is that correct?
- [Lukas] Yes. but...

257
00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:44.403
There it is again. "Yes. but..."

258
00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:51.070
"Yes. I held the red-level relic.
but it wasn't in my possession."

259
00:15:56.832 --> 00:16:00.209
If you don't deny the facts
of your case. Mr. Kyle.

260
00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:03.796
why are you requesting a
review of your sentence?

261
00:16:03.797 --> 00:16:05.715
[breathes deeply]

262
00:16:05.716 --> 00:16:11.054
Let me guess. The
reason. put simply. is...

263
00:16:12.931 --> 00:16:14.016
it's not fair.

264
00:16:15.726 --> 00:16:16.727
Would that be accurate?

265
00:16:22.399 --> 00:16:23.400
What's this made of?

266
00:16:24.651 --> 00:16:25.652
[Lukas scoffs]

267
00:16:27.446 --> 00:16:28.447
Metal.

268
00:16:28.989 --> 00:16:30.824
And where do we get the
ore to make the metal?

269
00:16:31.617 --> 00:16:34.744
- The mines.
- The mines are critical to our survival.

270
00:16:34.745 --> 00:16:40.745
[inhales sharply] But the work is hard and
it's dangerous and nobody wants to do it.

271
00:16:41.793 --> 00:16:43.836
So you have to do it.

272
00:16:43.837 --> 00:16:48.467
You and others like you who
have fallen afoul of the Pact.

273
00:16:50.802 --> 00:16:52.803
Just because you'll be
wearing yellow coveralls

274
00:16:52.804 --> 00:16:56.475
doesn't mean that you won't be
of vital service to the Silo.

275
00:17:00.437 --> 00:17:01.730
May I ask you a question?

276
00:17:05.692 --> 00:17:06.692
If you must.

277
00:17:06.693 --> 00:17:09.946
Why did you even grant this
request for a review? [scoffs]

278
00:17:10.989 --> 00:17:12.241
Agents. get out.

279
00:17:21.124 --> 00:17:22.484
- [door closes]
- Why do you think?

280
00:17:25.295 --> 00:17:26.879
To see if I knew what
was on the hard drive.

281
00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:27.964
No.

282
00:17:29.675 --> 00:17:33.011
[inhales sharply] I wanted to know
about the lights in the night sky.

283
00:17:35.597 --> 00:17:37.266
- [scoffs]
- What are they?

284
00:17:38.725 --> 00:17:40.769
- I don't know.
- Tell me what you think you know.

285
00:17:44.064 --> 00:17:45.315
They move.

286
00:17:47.568 --> 00:17:49.862
Actually. um. we move.

287
00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:55.075
We move?

288
00:17:55.701 --> 00:17:56.869
What do you think that means?

289
00:17:58.245 --> 00:18:03.041
I think we... [breathes
shakily] live on a... a ball.

290
00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:06.961
A-A massive one and it rotates.

291
00:18:06.962 --> 00:18:09.589
giving the impression
that the lights move.

292
00:18:09.590 --> 00:18:11.591
But they don't.

293
00:18:11.592 --> 00:18:12.885
It's the same with the sun.

294
00:18:13.552 --> 00:18:16.804
It doesn't move. we do.

295
00:18:16.805 --> 00:18:21.685
We spin. and that gives
us day and then night.

296
00:18:26.607 --> 00:18:30.819
[inhales sharply] The lights
in the sky are called stars.

297
00:18:31.820 --> 00:18:34.531
And they are massive
spheres of gas.

298
00:18:35.407 --> 00:18:40.078
And they shine from an
energy called radiation.

299
00:18:40.996 --> 00:18:45.124
Which makes them
perpetual balls of fire.

300
00:18:45.125 --> 00:18:46.293
[chuckles]

301
00:18:47.753 --> 00:18:49.378
And you're right.

302
00:18:49.379 --> 00:18:52.757
The world rotates

303
00:18:52.758 --> 00:18:58.722
and revolves around the sun.

304
00:19:00.766 --> 00:19:06.480
And the sun is just one of
billions of these stars.

305
00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:08.689
How do you know this? [scoffs]

306
00:19:08.690 --> 00:19:11.943
And... And wait. are there
actually other worlds that rev...

307
00:19:11.944 --> 00:19:13.153
Agents.

308
00:19:15.447 --> 00:19:18.199
Lukas Kyle. due to
your forthrightness

309
00:19:18.200 --> 00:19:21.452
and your willingness to
accept responsibility.

310
00:19:21.453 --> 00:19:25.791
I will amend your sentence
to five years in the mines.

311
00:19:26.375 --> 00:19:28.167
- May the Founders have mercy on you.
- [mutters]

312
00:19:28.168 --> 00:19:30.254
- Remove him.
- No. No. but... [scoffs]

313
00:19:32.673 --> 00:19:33.674
[door opens]

314
00:19:35.175 --> 00:19:36.176
[door closes]

315
00:19:37.135 --> 00:19:39.720
- [protester 1] Impeach Judge Meadows!
- [protester 2] Judge Meadows!

316
00:19:39.721 --> 00:19:40.805
[protester 1] Impeach her!

317
00:19:40.806 --> 00:19:42.515
[protester 2] Impeach
Judge Meadows!

318
00:19:42.516 --> 00:19:44.809
[clamoring]

319
00:19:44.810 --> 00:19:47.812
[protester 1] Impeach
Judge Meadows! Impeach her!

320
00:19:47.813 --> 00:19:51.900
- [protester 3] Impeach her!
- [protester 1] Impeach Judge Meadows!

321
00:19:53.151 --> 00:19:55.153
[water running]

322
00:19:55.946 --> 00:19:57.573
[distant chattering]

323
00:20:12.462 --> 00:20:14.547
It shouldn't rub
quite so much anymore.

324
00:20:14.548 --> 00:20:15.632
Thank you.

325
00:20:18.218 --> 00:20:19.219
[Carla] You all right?

326
00:20:21.305 --> 00:20:22.306
Why?

327
00:20:23.515 --> 00:20:26.101
You didn't come out of
your hole for 25 years.

328
00:20:28.604 --> 00:20:29.813
Now you're out all the time.

329
00:20:31.815 --> 00:20:32.816
Yeah.

330
00:20:35.319 --> 00:20:39.990
When I went up those stairs to get that
tape for Juliette. I was scared spitless.

331
00:20:42.201 --> 00:20:44.952
Then I saw you...
[clicks tongue]

332
00:20:44.953 --> 00:20:50.953
and I started thinking about
all those years I wasted.

333
00:20:52.461 --> 00:20:53.461
[sighs]

334
00:20:54.046 --> 00:20:55.088
We wasted.

335
00:20:56.882 --> 00:20:58.884
And I couldn't remember why.

336
00:20:59.968 --> 00:21:03.388
At least any why that
was worth a damn.

337
00:21:09.144 --> 00:21:12.231
[Carla breathes deeply]

338
00:21:17.986 --> 00:21:22.741
If Meadows goes for it. sending people
out to look around. come back...

339
00:21:24.993 --> 00:21:25.994
Yeah?

340
00:21:28.038 --> 00:21:29.164
Would you volunteer?

341
00:21:30.249 --> 00:21:31.250
In a heartbeat.

342
00:21:32.125 --> 00:21:33.627
- You?
- Yeah.

343
00:21:36.296 --> 00:21:37.376
What would you want to see?

344
00:21:39.633 --> 00:21:40.634
The distance.

345
00:21:49.726 --> 00:21:50.727
And Juliette.

346
00:21:59.945 --> 00:22:01.071
This not knowing...

347
00:22:02.906 --> 00:22:04.449
it's fucking killing me.

348
00:22:09.246 --> 00:22:10.622
I didn't think you liked her.

349
00:22:11.123 --> 00:22:13.250
She annoyed the living
shit out of me...

350
00:22:15.627 --> 00:22:17.880
but I knew that I could
trust her with my life.

351
00:22:18.630 --> 00:22:20.007
Looks like we got company.

352
00:22:22.551 --> 00:22:23.927
You gotta be kidding me.

353
00:22:26.847 --> 00:22:27.887
[Martha] I'll handle this.

354
00:22:29.141 --> 00:22:30.601
Deputy. is everything all right?

355
00:22:31.894 --> 00:22:33.102
You heading up to Judicial?

356
00:22:33.103 --> 00:22:35.521
Yeah. we got a right to
walk like everyone else.

357
00:22:35.522 --> 00:22:37.857
We're not here to stop you.
We're here to make sure

358
00:22:37.858 --> 00:22:39.483
- you get there safely.
- Why?

359
00:22:39.484 --> 00:22:41.485
Sheriff Billings asked us to.

360
00:22:41.486 --> 00:22:43.070
No. but why does he
think that we need...

361
00:22:43.071 --> 00:22:47.074
Because you may have a lot of
friends below here. but above...

362
00:22:47.075 --> 00:22:48.576
people might be throwing shit.

363
00:22:48.577 --> 00:22:50.453
[sighs] Literally.

364
00:22:50.454 --> 00:22:52.580
We'll peel off a couple
of levels below Judicial.

365
00:22:52.581 --> 00:22:56.667
[sighs] They see a sheriff's escort.
they might think we've chosen sides.

366
00:22:56.668 --> 00:23:00.379
Oh. so you'll leave us just when
things are likely to get bad?

367
00:23:00.380 --> 00:23:03.425
I mean. I'm a civilian. So yeah.

368
00:23:04.760 --> 00:23:06.177
Let's get on with it then.

369
00:23:06.178 --> 00:23:09.056
The. um. firebomb hit here.

370
00:23:10.641 --> 00:23:11.642
We all scattered.

371
00:23:12.309 --> 00:23:13.393
The fire didn't get inside.

372
00:23:14.102 --> 00:23:15.103
Where was the shooter?

373
00:23:16.146 --> 00:23:17.189
Right there.

374
00:23:17.940 --> 00:23:19.106
Retired raider.

375
00:23:19.107 --> 00:23:20.316
Reggie Smalls.

376
00:23:20.317 --> 00:23:22.027
Says he was just
reacting as trained.

377
00:23:22.611 --> 00:23:24.529
The asshole with the firebomb?

378
00:23:24.530 --> 00:23:25.613
Over there.

379
00:23:25.614 --> 00:23:28.867
A local kid Cooper was running
in to try and stop the guy.

380
00:23:30.827 --> 00:23:33.789
- Anybody recognize him?
- No.

381
00:23:35.165 --> 00:23:36.457
I'd like to see him.

382
00:23:36.458 --> 00:23:39.336
I wish you could. Judicial
hauled him up this morning.

383
00:23:39.920 --> 00:23:41.128
- They give a reason?
- No.

384
00:23:41.129 --> 00:23:44.465
When I asked. they just gave me that
look. you know that Judicial look.

385
00:23:44.466 --> 00:23:45.758
I used to be in Judicial.

386
00:23:45.759 --> 00:23:47.635
Oh. um. I'm sorry.

387
00:23:47.636 --> 00:23:50.389
- I didn't...
- No. I know that look only too well.

388
00:23:50.889 --> 00:23:51.890
Right.

389
00:23:53.559 --> 00:23:54.977
You still have Cooper's body?

390
00:23:58.564 --> 00:24:00.482
[Juliette] Okay. [sucks teeth]

391
00:24:05.320 --> 00:24:07.573
You can start. I
think that works.

392
00:24:08.407 --> 00:24:09.407
- Solo?
- Huh?

393
00:24:09.408 --> 00:24:11.201
Pump. Pump hard.

394
00:24:12.744 --> 00:24:13.911
[Juliette groans]

395
00:24:13.912 --> 00:24:16.164
Okay. that's not
good enough. Stop.

396
00:24:17.666 --> 00:24:20.001
Okay. I think if I... [sighs]

397
00:24:20.002 --> 00:24:22.879
- Maybe...
- I gotta go back to the vault.

398
00:24:22.880 --> 00:24:25.089
What? Why?

399
00:24:25.090 --> 00:24:26.883
I think I left it unlocked.

400
00:24:26.884 --> 00:24:28.843
Well. there's no one here.

401
00:24:28.844 --> 00:24:30.469
I think we're okay.

402
00:24:30.470 --> 00:24:31.971
No. I think I gotta go check it.

403
00:24:31.972 --> 00:24:34.182
No. no. no. it's okay. I
saw you. You locked it.

404
00:24:34.183 --> 00:24:35.850
- I don't think so.
- No. but you did.

405
00:24:35.851 --> 00:24:37.560
You closed the
door and it locked.

406
00:24:37.561 --> 00:24:40.021
- You're just saying that.
- No! Wait. what are you doing?

407
00:24:40.022 --> 00:24:41.607
You locked the vault.

408
00:24:43.317 --> 00:24:44.318
Okay?

409
00:24:45.652 --> 00:24:47.362
Yeah. I locked it.

410
00:24:48.906 --> 00:24:49.907
[sighs]

411
00:25:05.088 --> 00:25:06.840
[inhales sharply. coughs]

412
00:25:07.424 --> 00:25:09.176
[door opens]

413
00:25:12.012 --> 00:25:13.179
[door closes]

414
00:25:13.180 --> 00:25:14.556
Was it you?

415
00:25:15.140 --> 00:25:16.308
Absolutely not.

416
00:25:16.850 --> 00:25:20.938
I'm to believe that an effort to
impeach me just arose spontaneously?

417
00:25:22.064 --> 00:25:23.357
People are afraid.

418
00:25:24.024 --> 00:25:26.692
and they get more
afraid with every step

419
00:25:26.693 --> 00:25:29.403
that a group of Mechanicals
takes toward the air lock.

420
00:25:29.404 --> 00:25:31.197
You should have read the
letter that they sent.

421
00:25:31.198 --> 00:25:33.699
All they want is to
build better suits

422
00:25:33.700 --> 00:25:36.953
and to send a few people out
to explore and come back.

423
00:25:36.954 --> 00:25:38.162
Oh.

424
00:25:38.163 --> 00:25:40.998
[stammers] Just a
little exploration.

425
00:25:40.999 --> 00:25:42.458
Oh. that's...

426
00:25:42.459 --> 00:25:45.586
Everything. everything about
that is prohibited by the Pact.

427
00:25:45.587 --> 00:25:47.213
I wasn't going to agree to it.

428
00:25:47.214 --> 00:25:51.259
I was gonna say we'd form a
committee to study these ideas.

429
00:25:51.260 --> 00:25:52.678
[scoffs]

430
00:25:53.428 --> 00:25:55.346
And kill it with bureaucracy.

431
00:25:55.347 --> 00:25:56.682
Yes.

432
00:25:58.016 --> 00:25:59.643
[sighs]

433
00:26:01.353 --> 00:26:03.480
Uh. I'm...

434
00:26:04.690 --> 00:26:09.026
I'm sorry about the
impeachment talk.

435
00:26:09.027 --> 00:26:10.654
Look. I don't care.

436
00:26:13.365 --> 00:26:15.200
I'll be long gone
before it could happen.

437
00:26:17.578 --> 00:26:20.080
Would you come to.
um. my apartment?

438
00:26:21.206 --> 00:26:23.166
For dinner. Tonight.

439
00:26:24.835 --> 00:26:27.211
- Why?
- We need to talk about next steps.

440
00:26:27.212 --> 00:26:29.005
My next steps are
out the air lock.

441
00:26:29.006 --> 00:26:30.465
I'll have your suit.

442
00:26:31.967 --> 00:26:33.760
You can try it on.

443
00:26:40.976 --> 00:26:42.852
[inhales sharply] Come at 7:00?

444
00:26:42.853 --> 00:26:44.103
Mm-hmm.

445
00:26:44.104 --> 00:26:47.064
The. um. Mechanicals won't
be up here until 9:00.

446
00:26:47.065 --> 00:26:48.357
Okay.

447
00:26:48.358 --> 00:26:53.280
[breathes deeply]

448
00:26:53.989 --> 00:26:55.657
I spoke to Lukas Kyle.

449
00:26:56.283 --> 00:26:57.783
You're right. He is dangerous.

450
00:26:57.784 --> 00:27:01.120
Then why did you cut
his sentence in half?

451
00:27:01.121 --> 00:27:03.081
To make it seem like we're fair.

452
00:27:04.291 --> 00:27:05.333
Besides. someone like him.

453
00:27:05.334 --> 00:27:08.212
do you really think he could last
more than a year in the mines?

454
00:27:11.006 --> 00:27:14.091
[breathes deeply]

455
00:27:14.092 --> 00:27:16.762
[inhales sharply]
I'll see you tonight.

456
00:27:18.055 --> 00:27:21.057
If the mob don't grab me
and throw me over the rail.

457
00:27:21.058 --> 00:27:22.518
[chuckles]

458
00:27:32.236 --> 00:27:34.278
- [Juliette] Can you try again?
- [Solo] Yeah.

459
00:27:34.279 --> 00:27:36.364
[pumping]

460
00:27:36.365 --> 00:27:38.992
[Juliette] Yeah. Okay.
I think that'll work.

461
00:27:41.078 --> 00:27:42.246
Okay. [sighs]

462
00:27:54.967 --> 00:27:57.677
[buckles clicking]

463
00:27:57.678 --> 00:27:59.095
Are you okay?

464
00:27:59.096 --> 00:28:00.931
Yeah. Yeah.

465
00:28:01.849 --> 00:28:05.142
Yeah. I'll be fine. I'll just
have to get across the alleyway

466
00:28:05.143 --> 00:28:07.478
into the room and I'll be fine.

467
00:28:07.479 --> 00:28:08.938
I've done worse.

468
00:28:08.939 --> 00:28:12.483
Um. okay. so when I'm underwater.
just make sure you. um...

469
00:28:12.484 --> 00:28:14.402
Don't stop pumping. I know.

470
00:28:14.403 --> 00:28:15.946
Yeah. Okay.

471
00:28:17.865 --> 00:28:19.073
[mortician] <i>There's
no body. Hank.</i>

472
00:28:19.074 --> 00:28:20.741
Judicial took it.

473
00:28:20.742 --> 00:28:23.494
No. w-we don't want to see
the asshole with the firebomb.

474
00:28:23.495 --> 00:28:24.579
We wanna see Coop.

475
00:28:24.580 --> 00:28:26.247
No. that's what I'm telling you.

476
00:28:26.248 --> 00:28:27.666
They took Coop.

477
00:28:28.166 --> 00:28:29.167
What?

478
00:28:29.168 --> 00:28:30.293
Why?

479
00:28:30.294 --> 00:28:33.630
They said they were here for a body
and the only one here was Coop.

480
00:28:34.173 --> 00:28:36.132
- So they took him.
- No. no. no. no.

481
00:28:36.133 --> 00:28:37.842
I tried to explain.
but they were Judicial.

482
00:28:37.843 --> 00:28:39.468
There were two bodies
here yesterday.

483
00:28:39.469 --> 00:28:40.595
Not this morning.

484
00:28:40.596 --> 00:28:43.222
So. what happened?

485
00:28:43.223 --> 00:28:44.515
I don't know.

486
00:28:44.516 --> 00:28:45.850
You don't know?

487
00:28:45.851 --> 00:28:48.311
- Well. did you lock the door last night?
- Yes. I locked the door.

488
00:28:48.312 --> 00:28:50.188
- You think I'm an idiot?
- [Hank] I don't know. Juno.

489
00:28:50.189 --> 00:28:52.857
- You had two bodies here yesterday...
- The one that's missing. describe it.

490
00:28:52.858 --> 00:28:54.234
[sighs]

491
00:28:55.652 --> 00:28:58.447
It was a guy. about 40.

492
00:28:59.114 --> 00:29:00.114
Medium build.

493
00:29:00.115 --> 00:29:02.951
Um. big beard. Long hair.

494
00:29:11.335 --> 00:29:13.337
[pumping]

495
00:29:15.839 --> 00:29:17.174
[Juliette grunts]

496
00:29:19.343 --> 00:29:20.801
[sighs] Yeah.

497
00:29:20.802 --> 00:29:22.804
You'll be okay. Captain Nemo.

498
00:29:25.807 --> 00:29:28.227
[grunts. breathes heavily]

499
00:29:42.991 --> 00:29:45.619
[inhales deeply]

500
00:29:54.503 --> 00:29:56.255
[thuds. echoes]

501
00:30:11.353 --> 00:30:13.313
[inhales deeply]

502
00:30:31.081 --> 00:30:34.334
[inhales deeply]

503
00:30:50.184 --> 00:30:52.102
[muffled grunting]

